 UNO Refactoring Project Report

 Project Title

Refactoring and Modularization of a Console-Based UNO Game in Java

---

 1. Introduction

The purpose of this project was to improve the structure, readability, and maintainability of an existing console-based UNO game written in Java.
The original implementation contained tightly coupled logic, making the code difficult to maintain and extend.

To address these issues, the project was refactored into multiple specialized classes, each responsible for a single aspect of the program. This approach follows object-oriented design principles and improves separation of concerns.

The resulting application preserves the original game behavior while providing a cleaner and more organized codebase.

---

 2. Project Objectives

The main objectives of the refactoring process were:

* Improve code readability
* Reduce duplication and complexity
* Separate game responsibilities into dedicated classes
* Preserve original gameplay functionality
* Improve maintainability and future extensibility
* Follow clean coding practices

---

 3. Technologies Used

* Java
* Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)
* Console-based user interaction
* ArrayList collections
* Modular utility classes

---

 4. System Structure

The project was divided into several helper classes, each handling a specific responsibility.

 Main.java

The `Main` class acts as the central coordinator of the application.
It connects the helper classes together and provides simplified access to game-related operations.

Responsibilities:

* Program entry point
* Delegating actions to helper classes
* Managing shared scanner input

---

 CardRules.java

This class contains all logic related to UNO card interpretation and validation.

Responsibilities:

* Detecting card colors
* Detecting card ranks/types
* Extracting numeric values
* Verifying legal moves
* Calculating card point values

Examples of supported cards:

* `R7`
* `BS`
* `Y+2`
* `W`
* `W4`

The class centralizes rule validation, making the rest of the application simpler and more maintainable.

---

 BotStrategy.java

This class manages automated bot behavior.

Responsibilities:

* Selecting playable cards
* Prioritizing special cards
* Choosing the best color after wild cards
* Applying simple decision-making logic

The bot attempts to:

1. Play Draw Two cards first
2. Play Skip cards
3. Play numeric cards
4. Use Wild cards if necessary

This creates predictable and stable bot behavior while keeping the implementation simple.

---

 ConsolePrompter.java

This class handles all console-based interaction with the user.

Responsibilities:

* Requesting card choices
* Validating user input
* Requesting color selections
* Displaying input error messages

Separating input handling from game logic improves readability and follows clean architecture principles.

---

 ScoreCalculator.java

This utility class calculates final UNO scores.

Responsibilities:

* Summing remaining cards in losing players’ hands
* Ignoring the winning player’s hand
* Using UNO point rules for calculations

Point system:

* Number cards → face value
* Action cards → 20 points
* Wild cards → 50 points

---

 5. Refactoring Improvements

Several improvements were introduced during the refactoring process.

 5.1 Separation of Concerns

Each class now has a dedicated responsibility.
This reduces dependencies and makes debugging easier.

---

 5.2 Cleaner Main Class

The `Main` class was simplified significantly by moving logic into helper classes.

Benefits:

* Easier navigation
* Better readability
* Reduced complexity

---

 5.3 Reusable Utility Methods

Repeated logic was extracted into reusable methods.

Examples:

* Card legality checking
* Color extraction
* Rank detection
* Score calculations

---

 5.4 Improved Maintainability

The modular design allows future features to be added more easily, such as:

* Multiplayer support
* Graphical user interface
* Advanced AI behavior
* Additional UNO rules

---

 6. Challenges Encountered

Several challenges were addressed during the project:

 Input Validation

Handling invalid console input required repeated validation loops to prevent crashes.

 Rule Consistency

UNO legality rules had to remain consistent across both human and bot gameplay.

 Preserving Existing Behavior

The refactoring process needed to improve structure without changing the original gameplay mechanics.

---

 7. Testing

The refactored project was tested using various gameplay scenarios.

Tested functionality included:

* Legal and illegal card plays
* Wild card color selection
* Bot move selection
* Score calculation accuracy
* User input handling
* Edge-case validation

The refactored version preserved the intended functionality successfully.

---

 8. Conclusion

The project successfully transformed a monolithic UNO implementation into a modular and maintainable Java application.

The refactoring improved:

* Readability
* Code organization
* Reusability
* Maintainability

At the same time, the original gameplay behavior was preserved.

The final result demonstrates how applying object-oriented principles and modular design can significantly improve software quality even in relatively small projects.
